<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # 1 – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;"><em>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</em></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 2nd Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.4)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Exploring My Community</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> It's the Market!</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Identify keywords and phrases in texts. Reading: Identify simple story elements (e.g., characters, setting). Phonemic Awareness: Identify a spoken word's initial and final phoneme if these words have been previously encountered and are attached to a picture.</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can identify keywords and phrases related to community places in simple texts (e.g., "park" or "market"). Can identify simple story elements in community-related texts by locating characters and settings (e.g., recognizing "the girl" at "the library"). Can identify the initial and final phoneme of community-related words and their corresponding letters when supported by a picture (e.g., identifying the /ʈʃ/ sound at the beginning and /tʃ/ sound at the end of "church" with the letters "CH" and "CH" when shown a picture of a church).</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 30px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7; color: #1a5276; width: 35%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7; color: #1a5276; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7; color: #1a5276; width: 15%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher holds up large flashcards (from Lesson 1) depicting community places: "market," "park," "library," "church," "school." The teacher says, "Look! What is it?" Students call out the name. Teacher asks, "Where do we go to buy food?" (prompting "market"). Teacher shows a map of a simple community and points to places, asking "Where is the market?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models the pronunciation of target words while pointing to the flashcards. For "market," the teacher says "/ˈmɑːrkɪt/" clearly, emphasizing the /m/ sound. For "park," the teacher says "/pɑːrk/", emphasizing the /p/ sound. The teacher also models the "ch" sound /ʈʃ/ in "church" and points to the letters "CH" on a word card, showing initial and final letter correspondence. Teacher reads simple sentences, e.g., "It's a big park." while pointing to a picture of a park.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is this a school or a market?" (Teacher shows a picture of a market). (Expected answer: "Market!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can we play at the park? Yes or no?" (Expected answer: "Yes!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Does 'church' start with /ch/?" (Teacher points to 'ch' letters). (Expected answer: "Yes!")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Exploring My Community")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short, simple illustrated story (e.g., a Big Book or projected images with text) titled "My Trip to the Market." The story will feature a character, Mia, visiting different places in her community. The text will incorporate target vocabulary (e.g., "market," "park," "library," "big," "small," "see," "walk," "next to") and grammar (Present simple, "Where is it?," Adjectives + nouns).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Example Text (with pictures):</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: none; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Mia goes to the market.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The market is big.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">She sees fruits and vegetables.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Next to the market is a small park.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Mia walks to the park.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">She plays with a ball.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Where is the library? It's next to the park!</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Identifying the main idea and specific details from a very simple, illustrated narrative text about familiar topics.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> "Who goes to the market?" (Teacher points to Mia in the picture). (Expected answer: "Mia.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> Teacher reads statements and students show thumbs up (True) or thumbs down (False) based on the story. "The market is small." (False). "Mia sees fruits." (True).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching (pictures to words):</b> Teacher shows pictures of the market, park, library from the story. Students point to the corresponding word written on the board.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Reading)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Reading keywords and simple phrases, identifying story elements (characters, settings), and recognizing initial/final phonemes with letter correspondence. Grammar: WH-questions ("Where is it?"), Present simple ("It's the market."), Adjectives + nouns ("big park"). Vocabulary: park, market, school, library, church, store, home. Pronunciation: /p/ in "park," /m/ in "market," /ʈʃ/ (ch) in "church."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Keyword Circle:</b> The teacher distributes a handout with the story text and asks students to circle all the names of places (e.g., "market," "park," "library"). The teacher models first by circling "market." Students then circle "Mia" as the character.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Picture-Sentence Match & Phonics:</b> Students receive a worksheet with sentences from the story and corresponding pictures in a jumbled order. Students read each sentence (e.g., "The market is big." "She plays in the park.") and draw a line to match it to the correct picture. While matching, the teacher asks students to point to the first letter of words like "market" (M) and "park" (P), and to circle "CH" in "church" (if church is included in a similar sentence, e.g., "The library is near the church.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Read and Complete:</b> The teacher provides sentences with missing keywords from the story. Students read the sentence and fill in the blank with a word from a word bank, supported by small pictures next to the words. E.g., "Mia goes to the ______. (picture of market)" or "The park is ______. (picture of big)." The teacher checks for accurate reading and word choice.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Exploring My Community")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "Reading a Community Map." Students work individually or in pairs to read simple descriptions of places on a classroom "community map" poster and identify locations.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme: "It's the Market!" and Scenario: "Exploring My Community":</b> The teacher displays a large, colorful map of a simple community with labeled places (market, park, school, library, church, store). Beneath the map, there are 3-4 simple sentences about the map.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher gives each student a small worksheet with matching activities.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 1: Read and Point.</b> The teacher reads sentences aloud, and students point to the correct place on their individual small map or on the large classroom map. E.g., "This is the market. It is big." "The library is next to the church."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 2: Read and Draw.</b> Students read short sentences provided on their worksheet and draw a simple symbol (e.g., a circle, a star) over the correct place on a simplified map on their worksheet. E.g., "Circle the market." "Draw a star on the church." For words like "church," students are asked to underline the "CH" letters.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 3: Keyword Search.</b> Students read a short text (e.g., "My friend Leo goes to the school. He likes the big park near his home.") and underline the keywords (Leo, school, park, home). They then identify the initial phoneme of "park" and final phoneme of "school" by circling the corresponding letter(s).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the "Read and Draw" activity and review of student worksheets. The teacher notes if students can correctly identify and match places based on written descriptions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students receive a worksheet with 3-4 simple sentences about community places and small pictures. Students read each sentence and draw a line to the correct picture. For two specific words in the sentences (e.g., "market" and "church"), they will circle the initial and final letters that correspond to the identified phonemes (e.g., 'M' for /m/ in 'market', 'CH' for /ʈʃ/ at the beginning and end of 'church').</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can identify keywords (e.g., "market," "park," "library") in simple texts.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can match simple sentences to corresponding pictures correctly.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can identify initial and final phonemes/letters of target words (e.g., /m/ in market, /p/ in park, /ʈʃ/ in church) when supported by a picture.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete an exit ticket by coloring a happy face, neutral face, or sad face next to these statements:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can read the word 'market'."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can find the 'P' sound in 'park'."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new place did I read about today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of reading the story was easy for me?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What reading strategies worked best to help students identify keywords and story elements (e.g., picture support, repeated reading, chunking sentences)?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Were the texts and tasks appropriately challenging for Pre-A1.4 learners? Did students successfully identify the initial and final phonemes with letter correspondence?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for students who struggled with identifying story elements or phoneme-letter connections? How can I reinforce these reading skills in future lessons?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> "In our next lesson, we will use these words and phrases about community places to talk with a partner. We will practice asking and answering 'Where is it?' questions about our community map!"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">3-5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>